lesson: 152
title: The whole from its fraction
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: If we know how much a fraction of something is, we can find the whole.
explain: Divide by the number above the line to get one part, then multiply by the number below the line to get all the parts.
explain: Three fourths of a ribbon is 18 cm: one fourth is 18 ÷ 3 = 6 cm, and the whole ribbon is 4 × 6 = 24 cm.
vars: a=name k=3..20 p=3..10 m=1..9
need: m < p
need: (m % 2 != 0 || p % 2 != 0) && (m % 3 != 0 || p % 3 != 0) && (m % 5 != 0 || p % 5 != 0)
answer: p * k
exercise: {m}/{p} of a ribbon is {m*k} cm long. How long is the whole ribbon in centimetres?
exercise: {a} has read {m*k} pages, which is {m}/{p} of the book. How many pages does the book have?
exercise: There are {m*k} girls in a school choir. The girls are {m}/{p} of the choir. How many children are in the choir?
exercise: {a} spent {m*k} dollars. That was {m}/{p} of {a.his} money. How many dollars did {a.he} have?
exercise: A tank is {m}/{p} full and holds {m*k} litres now. How many litres does the full tank hold?
test: How many pages are in a book if {m}/{p} of it is {m*k} pages?
test: A train has gone {m*k} km, and that is just {m}/{p} of its route. How long is the whole route in kilometres?
test: {a} paid {m*k} dollars for a game, which was {m}/{p} of what {a.he} had saved. How many dollars had {a.he} saved?
test: If {m}/{p} of a number is {m*k}, what is the number?
exercise: {m*k} pupils ride the bus to a school in Ohio. They are {m}/{p} of all its pupils. How many pupils does the school have?
exercise: A road crew in Kent has paved {m*k} m of a road, which is {m}/{p} of its length. How long is the road in metres?
exercise: What is the whole, if {m}/{p} of it is {m*k}?
exercise: {a} gave away {m*k} stickers, which were {m}/{p} of all {a.his} stickers. How many stickers did {a.he} have?
exercise: A jug holds {m*k} cl when it is {m}/{p} full. How many centilitres does it hold when it is full?
exercise: In a park in Melbourne, {m*k} trees are gum trees, and they make up {m}/{p} of all the trees. How many trees are in the park?
exercise: {a} has walked {m*k} km, which is {m}/{p} of a trail in New Zealand. How long is the trail in kilometres?
exercise: A shop in Toronto has sold {m*k} T-shirts, that is {m}/{p} of the T-shirts it had. How many T-shirts did the shop have at first?
test: A number's {m}/{p} is {m*k}. What is the whole number?
test: {m*k} euros is {m}/{p} of the price of a bike in Dublin. What does the bike cost in euros?
test: {a} has done {m*k} sums, which are {m}/{p} of the homework. How many sums are in the homework?
test: In a box of crayons, {m*k} are broken, and these are {m}/{p} of the crayons. How many crayons are in the box?
test: A plane over Canada has flown {m*k} km, only {m}/{p} of its flight. How many kilometres is the whole flight?
test: How many litres does a full tank hold if {m}/{p} of it is {m*k} litres?
exercise: {m}/{p} of the ribbon's {m*k} cm. how long's the whole thing
exercise: {a} read {m*k} pages, that's {m}/{p} of the book. how many pages in the book
exercise: my mate says it's {m*k} ÷ {m} = {k} pages. nah that's only 1/{p}. {m*k} pages is {m}/{p} of the book, whole book?
exercise: {m}/{p} of the trip is {m*k+5} km — no wait, {m*k} km. how long's the whole trip
test: done {m*k} m of the fence, that's {m}/{p} of it, took 2 days btw. how long's the fence
test: {a} spent {m*k} dollars, which was {m}/{p} of {a.his} money. how much did {a.he} have to start
test: {m}/{p} of a book = {m*k} pages. how many pages whole book
exam: It is given that {m}/{p} of a ribbon measures {m*k} cm. Determine the length of the entire ribbon in centimetres.
exam: Whereas {a} has hitherto read {m*k} pages, constituting {m}/{p} of the volume, ascertain the total number of pages of which the volume consists.
exam: Ascertain the number of which {m}/{p} is equal to {m*k}.
exam: A sum of {m*k} dollars, representing {m}/{p} of the savings of {a}, is expended upon a gift. State the total amount of the savings of {a} prior to the said expenditure, in dollars.
exercise: When Great-uncle Joe passed away, {a} got {m*k} dollars, which was {m}/{p} of all the money he left. How many dollars did he leave?
exercise: {a}'s mum was laid off and now gets {m*k} dollars a week, which is {m}/{p} of her old pay. What was her old weekly pay in dollars?
test: {a} fibbed, said {a.he}'d read the whole thing. pfft, read {m*k} pages, that's {m}/{p} of the book. how many pages has the bloody book got
